 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The fused approach reduces the number of kernel launches and memory accesses, leading to a small speedup [S1, unofficial]. On Mip-NeRF360 outdoor scenes with an RTX 4090, it reduces training time from 5m31s to 5m04s [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** It reduces VRAM requirements because no additional buffers for parameters are necessary during the update step [S1, unofficial]. On the same hardware and scenes, VRAM usage drops from 6.0GiB to 5.6GiB [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Significant VRAM reduction, especially beneficial for large scenes with many Gaussians, and a small but consistent speedup [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** The attainable performance improvements are reduced compared to skipping updates for invisible Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. After integration, parameter updates remain the main bottleneck of training [S1, unofficial]. It also reduces extensibility compared to a modular optimizer design [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When VRAM is a limiting factor or when a small speedup is desired without sacrificing the correctness of the Adam optimizer [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When maximum training speed is the primary goal and VRAM is sufficient; in such cases, skipping updates for invisible Gaussians (with its associated trade-offs) might be preferred [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduces training time by eliminating the overhead of a separate optimizer kernel launch and memory transfers. On Mip-NeRF360 outdoor scenes with an RTX 4090, it reduces training time from 5m31s to 5m04s [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduces VRAM requirements because no additional buffers for parameters are necessary during the update step. On the same hardware and scenes, it reduces VRAM from 6.0GiB to 5.6GiB [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Provides a small speedup and VRAM reduction over the full version but reduces extensibility [S1, unofficial]. Attainable performance improvements are reduced compared to skipping updates for invisible Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Lower VRAM usage, slightly faster training, reduced kernel launch overhead.
+*   **Limitations:** Reduced extensibility, parameter updates remain the main bottleneck, requires updating zero-gradient parameters which limits potential speedups from skipping invisible Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When VRAM is constrained and slight training speedups are desired without sacrificing the correctness of the Adam optimizer's internal state.
+*   **When not to use:** When maximum training speed is required and the potential quality degradation or inconsistency from skipping updates for invisible Gaussians is acceptable (in which case, combining with skipping updates is recommended) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Fused Backward and Optimizer technique was introduced in the context of improving the efficiency of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) training [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the observation that the GPU spends 40% to 60% of total training time on the optimized Adam update routine in the original 3DGS implementation [S1, unofficial]. The method was analyzed and improved in "Faster-GS: Analyzing and Improving Gaussian Splatting Optimization" [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced in "Faster-GS: Analyzing and Improving Gaussian Splatting Optimization" [S1, unofficial]. The authors identified that the GPU spends between 40% and 60% of the total training time on the optimized Adam update routine in the original 3DGS implementation [S1, unofficial]. This fusion technique was proposed to alleviate this bottleneck by integrating the optimizer step directly into the rasterizer's backward pass [S1, unofficial].
 
